Open items. What is done lives in git history.

Transport
- [ ] ZDT: at 64 B under loss, retransmits outnumber NAKs about seven to one: timeouts fire on datagrams whose acks were merely held by a busy receiver. Throughput sits at the window cap regardless, so this costs bandwidth, not rate. A receiver holding acks for a whole verdict window likewise reads as a queue, which costs the odd 1 KiB rep. Both want the ack to report how long it was held, QUIC style, and the sender to subtract it; a wire change.
- [ ] ZDT: the queueing test is relative, so at microsecond round trips (loopback, same rack) the ordinary cost of data in flight reads as a queue and pins the window low. An absolute margin opens it there but costs ~30% at 64 B; see ZDTRttEstimator::IsQueueing.
- [ ] Graceful disconnects (?)
- [ ] Feature flag negotiation (?)

P2P
- [ ] IPv6 hole punching. Gather is IPv4-only (LocalCandidates uses GetLocalAddresses(IPv4) and the Host opens one family); gather v6 host candidates, offer them, and punch over v6, dual-stack or a second socket. The reflector already round-trips v6 addresses. Untestable locally for now.
- [ ] NAT-type detection is in znet: two reflectors on distinct IPs yield GatherResult.nat_type (agree is endpoint-independent, differ is address-dependent i.e. symmetric), a symmetric verdict drops relay_delay so the punch goes straight to relay, and a sequential mapping is extended by PredictedPorts. Open: the game master advertises only its embedded relay, so advertise a distinct-IP second reflector to game peers (a second relay host plus a reflector-list field on the register/response path) or their NAT stays Unknown. Field validation of the verdict and prediction needs a real symmetric NAT.
- [ ] STUN (RFC 5389) Binding so public STUN servers work as reflectors, not only our RelayServer. Optional.
- [ ] Connection migration: finish it on the wire foundation (4aefad0). Done: kFlagHasCid + an 8-byte connection id (the sender's guid) in the online header, PathChallenge/PathResponse offline messages, ZDTOptions::enable_connection_migration to emit the id, header round-trip tests. Remaining, in order: (1) server backend routes by the peer's guid on an address miss and, on a known id from a new address, sends a PathChallenge and only moves the session's send target and re-keys routes_ once a PathResponse comes back from that address; (2) the challenged side replies to a PathChallenge (client backend, off its own socket); (3) the client can rebind its socket to a new local address without dropping the session, which is the real migration trigger and the genuinely tricky part (a receive thread is parked on the old fd); (4) the same routing in p2p::Host; (5) the version gate or a handshake capability bit so a migration-capable peer never stamps ids at one that cannot parse them, then bump the protocol; (6) a loopback rebind test end to end. Security: the id is a random 64-bit guid (unguessable off-path) and the path challenge proves liveness before the send target moves, but an on-path attacker who sees the id and answers from a new address can redirect a session's egress (a DoS, not a data leak, since payloads stay AEAD-protected); document it and consider a token-keyed tag. Repunch is the fallback where re-pathing cannot recover.
- [ ] Relay: per-allocation rate or byte limit, so one pairing cannot use a relay host's whole uplink; only probes are throttled today.
- [ ] Relay: a remote allocation protocol, so a broker can use a relay on another host instead of embedding RelayServer; a standalone relay binary follows from it.

Security
- [ ] Audit the encryption layer.
- [ ] Validate the peer's DH public key: d2i_PUBKEY takes whatever arrives and EVP_PKEY_derive_set_peer does not check it, so a small-order key passes. EVP_PKEY_public_check covers what OpenSSL's parameter check does not.

Performance
- [ ] ZDTInbox allocates one right-sized Buffer per datagram; a freelist recycling drained buffers would make the steady state allocation-free.
- [ ] ZDT delivery copies every record into its own Buffer (OnRecord); records could be owning slices of one per-datagram backing store. Matters most for many small messages.
- [ ] Buffer's data_ could use malloc/realloc so growth happens in place. Contained, but low payoff while the hot paths reuse scratch buffers.

Benchmarks
- [ ] CPU cost per message, once it can be compared honestly: only znet-bench builds and parses an application message where the others hand a pointer to the library, and process-wide CPU charges the harness's own spin to whichever library pumps cheaply. Give the comparison benches equivalent work and measure per thread.
- [ ] Memory profiling.

Tooling and docs
- [ ] Sanitizers in CI (ASan/UBSan, TSan) behind a ZNET_SANITIZE option; CI is Release only, so the Buffer asserts never run there either.
- [ ] Fuzz testing.
- [ ] Document thread-safety guarantees for every public API.
- [ ] Proxy protocol v2 support.
- [ ] Saving a packet buffer to a file.
- [ ] More events; better error handling.
